NOTES.update({
241:'Pellerin Bataille de Karahasan is a 29.4×40 cm stenciled lithograph, number 154, last-quarter nineteenth century. The 29 August 1877 caption dates the battle; no exact printing date is inferred.',
243:'Pellerin Dutch comic sheet is one 40.1×29.5 cm lithograph carrying both Angeli number 123 and Moloch number 123bis stories. It differs from the smaller French Angeli-only 19.8×29.6 cm sheet, candidate 133, in language, layout, second composition and inventory. Two printed narratives on this one sheet count once.',
245:'Pellerin Prise de Constantine is a 41.8×61.8 cm woodcut with accompanying song, source 1837. The no-song issue is separately described; Raffet’s existing smaller ink/watercolor drawing and Boulay/Deckherr woodcut have different makers and dimensions. CP 1910 remains a catalogue reference.',
247:'Pellerin Notre-Dame protectrice contre le choléra is a 39.3×29.9 cm lithograph, source 1884–1890, after earlier woodcut imagery associated with Maurin. Related Santé/Clarté/Vernay titles are versions, not interchangeable physical sheets; crossed publisher numbers 259/260 remain literal evidence.',
248:'Pellerin/Réveillé Saint Thomas is a 41×32.2 cm woodcut, source circa 1855, with specific heading and songs. Use of the same block for Saint Barthélemy and separately documented later lithographic/reprint versions does not turn this explicitly titled woodcut into those objects.',
249:'Pellerin/Vanson Soumission d’Abd-el-Kader is a 42.1×64.1 cm woodcut dated 1848. The history distinguishes an earlier issue by different accompanying text; current and legacy inventories identify one sheet, not two.',
250:'Pellerin/Wendling Sainte Geneviève is a 41.9×63.9 cm woodcut dated 1837. Named maker, large landscape format, block dimensions and inventory distinguish the Glémarec woodcut and smaller Gangel/Didion lithographs. Printing blocks in MUCEM and Épinal are separate objects.',
251:'Charles Pellerin Saint Hubert is a 44×29.4 cm crayon lithograph number 12, source 1880–1887, with collector F. Albert stamp. The earlier 1859 issue and other makers’ woodcut versions are related imagery, not the same impression.',
252:'Charles Pinot’s catalogued historical slavery scene is a 21.4×34 cm lithographic notebook-cover sheet, source 1872–1875. Historical title and content remain literal; the publisher’s personal death year is not used to change the supplied workshop printing range.',
254:'Guillaume s’en va-t’en guerre is a 39.7×27.4 cm lithograph from Pinot-Sagaire, Actualité number 30, source circa 1866. Charles Pinot’s design attribution stays qualified, with no invented exact year.',
255:'Pomel/Saint-Fal/Boulard Richard Cœur de Lion is narrative sheet number 4, a 25.9×32.8 cm intaglio dated 1812–1818. Scene, plate number and inventory distinguish numbers 1 and 2; their shared Jouy textile reference does not combine the sheets.',
256:'Rabier-Boulard Vrai portrait du Juif errant is a 40.5×32.3 cm woodcut with Orléans Rue des Carmes imprint, source 1813–1837. Historical title and narrative date 1784 remain source content. MUCEM exemplar and reversed Roiné/Dumoutier/Leloup or Huet-Perdoux versions are explicitly distinct copies and impressions.',
257:'Pellerin Saint Joseph is a 42.4×31.3 cm woodcut after Guido Reni and an anonymous intaglio, source circa 1837. Houston’s oil painting is the source composition, not this print. Gibault Gustave’s named 1868 Ascension annotation remains later manuscript evidence rather than an exact printing date; other makers’ Saint Joseph paintings/prints are distinct.',
258:'Ryckebusch/Pellerin Grands soldats américains is one 40×29.7 cm lithographic portrait sheet, number 109, circa 1918. September 1918 refers to the preparatory drawing submission, while the separate May 1999 acquisition date is not creation. Multiple portraits do not multiply artwork count.',
259:'Ryckebusch Grands soldats belges is one 40×29.6 cm lithographic sheet, number 107, circa 1918. Source maker says Imagerie d’Épinal while the imprint names Pellerin; both are retained. Different national group, publication number and inventory distinguish the American and English sheets.',
260:'Ryckebusch/Pellerin Grands soldats anglais is one 39.9×29.4 cm lithographic portrait sheet, number 106, circa 1918. It is physically separate from numbers 107 and 109, with its own library mark and inventory, and retains a print type despite its preparatory-drawing relationship.'
})
